WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:01.740
Christina: Camille, welcome to AEI!

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.340
Camille: It's great to be back, Christina.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.430
Christina: Oh, my goodness, do you know that
21 years ago, I don't know if it was today,

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:13.639
but close to today we were interviewed by
Ben Wattenberg, for the AEI program Think

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:19.970
Tank which aired on PBS, and the question
was, "Has feminism gone too far?" and I looked

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:26.109
at the transcript and it's amazing, because
all of the things we were saying are truer

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:28.240
today than they were 21 years ago...

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.240
Camille: Absolutely.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.800
Christina: ...about the excesses of feminism,
and the victimology, the male bashing, the

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.829
panic over sexuality.

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:40.920
At the time, 21 years ago we were confident
that it would be corrected.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.910
It was simply too, too mad to succeed, but
it's still here.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:47.910
Camille: I know.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.390
Well, Christina, we sent up early warnings.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:56.899
You, heroically in the late 1980s, were in
direct confrontation with women studies professors,

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:58.050
at a time when...

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.050
Christina: At a time when, as philosophers...

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.600
Camille: Yes, at a time when I could not get
published and finally after my first book,

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:08.240
Sexual Personae was released in 1990, you
wrote to me, and told me of your problems.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:13.659
And we'd discovered each other as allies,
and that our status, it seems to me as truth

00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:16.560
tellers, is historically unchallenged.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.479
And I think that people are going to start
to wake up and to realize, if they had listened

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:22.159
to us at the beginning, we wouldn't have these
problems now.

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:28.469
Christina: We wouldn't, and it was so obvious
to me, I was criticizing the feminist theorists

00:01:28.469 --> 00:01:34.609
at the American Philosophical Association,
and what they were writing at the time was

00:01:34.609 --> 00:01:36.049
just warmed over Marxism.

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:44.499
It was so much animus to American society,
to men, to everything, to art, to beauty and

00:01:44.499 --> 00:01:48.259
it seemed to me, it wouldn't take long for
it to correct itself.

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:53.520
It was never corrected, and what happened
is you and I...we did...I believe won the

00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:00.289
argument, and the journalists agreed with
us, and the court of public opinion was on

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:01.739
our side.

00:02:01.739 --> 00:02:05.000
They won all the assistant professorships.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.139
They just quietly worked.

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:10.849
They didn't care about criticism, because
all criticism is by definition backlash.

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:15.680
Camille: Well, a lot of people dropped out
of graduate school, the really free thinkers.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.490
The independent minds couldn't take the kind
of conformism and the PC ideology that they

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:22.989
had to account out to.

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:24.560
I got letters like that.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.200
Once I was known in the early '90s, letters
came from all over the country from former

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.129
graduate students who said they knew they
had no future in the profession, because of

00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:39.930
a total, kind of brain dead attitude you had,
a servile, sicko fantasy that actually you

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.030
had to have to certain received opinions.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:48.790
Christina: Absolutely and in our interview
21 years ago you mentioned this Kremlin-esque

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:49.810
feeling in feminism.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:56.480
It was a Kremlin-like environment, so much
censorship and excommunication, and so forth,

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:03.499
and students today don't realize there had
been this purge of anyone who was a dissident.

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:09.209
I happened to have tenure when I started criticizing
it, but it would be career diminishing or

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:13.019
career annihilating to criticize feminism,
even more so today.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:14.019
Camille: Right.

00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:18.829
Well, when I was a graduate student at Yale
which was 1968 to 1972, I was the only person

00:03:18.829 --> 00:03:24.959
doing a dissertation on sex at the graduate
school, and I had trouble getting a job because

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:25.959
of it.

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:28.609
I was the only openly gay person at the Yale
Graduate School.

00:03:28.609 --> 00:03:32.420
There were a lot of closeted people, and I
didn't have a sex life, okay?

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.129
I thought it was important to me to put the
gay identity out there.

00:03:36.129 --> 00:03:44.090
But from the very start, as feminism's ideology
cohered and solidified, I could not have a

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.000
single conversation with feminists, either
inside or outside the Academy.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.640
They could not stand...again, just what you
say...anti-art, anti-rock 'n roll, anti-Rolling

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.640
Stones.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.760
They would not take seriously anything that
has to do with biology and hormones or anything

00:03:58.760 --> 00:03:59.760
like that.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.110
I was completely ostracized, but I belonged
to the pro-sex wing of feminism, also pro

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:09.560
art, pro beauty, pro fashion, pro Hollywood,
all of these things were anathema.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:10.560
Christina: Pro glamor.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.560
Camille: Pro glamor.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.659
But then thanks to Madonna, there was this
new dissident wing in the '90s, which won.

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:17.090
The pro-sex wing of feminism won.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:18.090
Christina: Right.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:24.009
Camille: But now we won all these gains, but
the institutionally entrenched, feminist commissars,

00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:28.200
all right, have brought it all back to square
one again.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.270
All the new freedoms we won in the '90s, in
the pro-sex insurgency are gone again.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:41.199
Christina: It's amazing, and it's a kind of
axis of intolerance with deans, and fanatical

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:45.560
young feminists, and the United States government.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:50.050
Because the Department of Education has sent
this directive to all the schools, that they

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:57.449
have to clamp down on harassers, and they've
defined it so broadly that it includes if

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:01.630
you overhear someone telling an off-color
joke, you can bring them up on charges of

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:02.630
harassment.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:04.610
So we have this little reign of terror, this
hyper puritanism.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:10.090
It's as though George Orwell's Junior Anti-Sex
League has occupied feminism.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.630
Camille: It's truly Stalinist, absolutely.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:18.580
Now I take a very extreme view which is that
colleges and universities should never interfere

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.350
in any way with the social lives of the students.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.699
It's up to the students to regulate themselves.

00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:27.419
If a crime is truly committed, it must go
to the police, so a lot of these problems

00:05:27.419 --> 00:05:33.840
with stuff if universities would confine themselves
to education and not to the surveillance of

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.860
student's private lives, now our generation
won these freedoms.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.379
It was in loco parentis when we arrived in
college.

00:05:40.379 --> 00:05:42.740
Christina: Yes, parietal hours.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:47.770
Camille: And men wouldn't be allowed in your
room, and then if they were there...this was

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:52.419
right before I got to college...but you had
to have your feet on the ground at one time.

00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:53.990
They'd look through your door.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:55.389
Christina: People managed to do things.

00:05:55.389 --> 00:06:01.380
Camille: In 1964, when I arrived in college
our women's dorm, the girls had to sign in

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.530
at 11:00 o'clock at night whereas the men
could run free all night long.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:09.482
So we are the ones who demanded that the authority
figures get out of our sex lives.

00:06:09.482 --> 00:06:12.009
Christina: And we wanted to be as free and
as wild as the boys.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:14.970
Camille: Yes, and they said to us, "No, the
world is dangerous.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:20.280
We need to protect you from rape," and what
we said was, "Give us the freedom to risk

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.280
rape."

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.320
These are the freedoms that we won, and now
this younger generation of girls want the

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.789
parental figures back in.

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:28.970
It is deeply Stalinist.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:30.520
It is deeply infantilizing.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.120
It's deeply reactionary.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:34.370
It has nothing to do with feminism.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:35.370
Christina: It's fainting couch.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:36.370
Camille: This is a retreat.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:37.370
Christina: It's fainting couch feminism.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:38.370
Camille: Victorian.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.370
Christina: Victorian ladies...

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:40.370
Camille: Yes, it is.

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:44.570
Christina: ...swooning on this chaise, and
you've heard about the Safe Spaces young women.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:51.229
I gave a lecture at Oberlin and Georgetown
a year ago and they organized Safe Spaces

00:06:51.229 --> 00:06:58.180
for students who were triggered by...it was
a philosophy professor was going to reduce

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:04.629
PTSD because of what they objected to, is
that I might have some ideas that invalidate

00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:05.629
their experience.

00:07:05.629 --> 00:07:07.379
Camille: They're institutionalizing neurosis.

00:07:07.379 --> 00:07:08.379
It's what they're doing.

00:07:08.379 --> 00:07:09.379
Christina: That's absolutely true.

00:07:09.379 --> 00:07:10.379
Camille: It's absolute madness.

00:07:10.379 --> 00:07:15.919
Christina: Have you heard the phrase, the
fashion on campus now is called intersectional

00:07:15.919 --> 00:07:19.229
feminism, and it's supposedly very progressive?

00:07:19.229 --> 00:07:26.479
And it's going to take feminism to a place
where it is taking account of women with multiply

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:28.830
marginalized identities, on and on.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:35.550
But all of the women, the practitioners, seem
to meet at the intersection of propaganda,

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:39.939
and neurosis, and rage, and they're bullying
people.

00:07:39.939 --> 00:07:41.169
They're using feminism now.

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:42.849
They're telling men, "Check your privilege."

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:49.509
Camille: Well, I think it's a good idea that
feminism be critiqued by class issues.

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:56.070
Because what I have said all along is that
what is being demanded is a bourgeois protection,

00:07:56.070 --> 00:08:03.729
a white middle-class girl style, the perfect
little girl emerging from her parents living

00:08:03.729 --> 00:08:06.990
room wants the same kind of protections on
the college campus.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:10.810
So I've always felt there was a bourgeois
element in this that was never sufficiently

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:11.810
critiqued.

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:16.189
And then what I see on the street are Latino
women, African-American women who may be middle-class,

00:08:16.189 --> 00:08:20.069
but have a working class background, as I
did as an Italian from an immigrant family...who

00:08:20.069 --> 00:08:23.520
were able to handle themselves on the street,
able to speak back.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.190
Don't mind having strong voices and strong
gestures.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:29.370
Christina: Well, intersectionality started
with those women.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:34.650
So it was a group of African-American women
who were just setup with the Women's Movement,

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:42.620
attending only the needs of highly professional,
upper middle-class women, but it has been

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:46.860
appropriated by mostly middle-class white
women on the campus.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:53.740
So Safe Space was actually a word that was
used early in Black Feminism, as where black

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:59.250
women and Hispanic...places where they could
just talk to each other and they weren't censored.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:00.760
They could speak their minds.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:05.600
But now it means a place where, for example
young women at Brown University if there's

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.630
a debate that upsets them, they flee to a
Safe Space where they...It was described in

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.110
The New York Times by Judith Shulevitz.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:19.800
They had coloring books, crayons, tapes of
frolicking puppies, bubbles and this is what

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:20.900
feminism has come to.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.410
Camille: It's what education has come to.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:28.030
Absolutely the definition down, they're therapeutic.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:29.030
What can I say?

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:32.930
Therapeutic resorts is what education has
turned into.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:35.420
Christina: So they have feeling circles.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:46.360
They have reflection journals, and it's this
odd mix of therapy and anger because the word

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:52.590
for this style of activism, they call them
Cry Bullies because they combine...they're

00:09:52.590 --> 00:10:01.520
both victim and victimizer and they do to
other people this new style of campus intersectionality.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:05.670
They actually do to other people what they
claim was done to them.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:12.381
They treat people...they objectify and they
stereotype, and they demonize and then they

00:10:12.381 --> 00:10:13.790
try to get people punished.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:15.990
Camille: Well, Christina, this is coming from
the public schools.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:21.080
It's coming from the public school education,
which was devoid of knowledge of world history

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.630
and world geography.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:30.640
It is nothing but naval gazing and the diversity
training, and all kinds of instructions about

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:38.030
social interaction, when in fact, if the atrocities
of history were revealed to young people,

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:41.800
they would be able to put their own little
hurt feelings into perspective.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:42.800
Christina: Into perspective.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:43.800
They have no perspective.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:44.800
Camille: No.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.170
Christina: I think with these anti-bullying
programs, they are learning that even the

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:51.600
slightest insult is a major event.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:52.660
You must bring in the adults.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:53.660
"It was terrible.

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:58.220
He called me a name," so they come to college
thinking that if someone disagrees with them

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:01.670
or says something that's distressing, that
it's a catastrophe.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:06.270
Camille: I've called my feminism Amazon feminism.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:10.590
I've called it Drag Queen feminism, Street
Smart feminism because the drag queens of

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:13.240
old knew how to handle themselves on the street.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:18.110
They could be a six-foot tall, African-American
guy in a dress and he knew how to pull off

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:23.610
his high heels, and defend himself on the
street, and what a tongue they had.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:27.280
And now everything is about weakening students,
not strengthening them.

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:30.770
Christina: Oh, it's exactly that and now let's
talk about the young men.

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:33.920
Can you imagine being a young man on campus
today?

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:39.250
And what I see happening is it's just the
way society used to police the sexuality of

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:40.320
gay people.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:44.870
It's now open season on the sexuality of heterosexual
males.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:45.870
Camille: Absolutely.

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:47.540
Christina: They're monitored, and policed,
and demonized.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:48.540
Camille: ...and neutered.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:49.540
They're neutered.

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:51.790
By the time they get to the Ivy League, they're
neutered.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:53.830
Everything's been stripped off of them.

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:57.160
They've been made to feel ashamed of anything
virile.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:58.640
They have nothing to identify with.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.040
Christina: They're taught their masculinity
is a pathology in need of a cure and then

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:06.720
it's offered to them in these gender studies
programs, and it's not just gender studies.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:14.770
Now this philosophy that demonizes men, it's
throughout curriculum and you're absolutely

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.150
right, it's going on in the high schools.

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:20.950
Camille: It's coming early on and so then
can you wonder, "Why are video games so violent?

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:23.560
Why is there so much porn on the web?"

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:24.560
Why?

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:31.220
It's because the imagination is being repressed,
and censored, and forcibly cleansed of anything

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.630
that's authentically animalistic or sexy or
vibrant.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:40.280
So you have all the imaginative energy of
the universe now going into these other forms

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:44.100
where it becomes much more violent, and when
people see it, they go, "Oh, this is atrocious.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:45.150
This is so awful."

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:46.890
But guess what?

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.280
You've made a whole area of life impermissible.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:54.280
You've made all kinds of human desires and
fantasies impermissible and so it has to come

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:56.020
out someplace and that's where it is.

00:12:56.020 --> 00:13:01.080
Christina: That's right, and what happened
last year was a group of cultural critics

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:09.290
from, I guess, liberal arts colleges started
writing for magazines for video games, I mean

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:10.290
websites.

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:14.290
And these cultural critiques started to attack
the gamers, and tell them their games were

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:20.060
sexist, and that they were contributing to
the rape culture, and the gamers were not

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.640
like the young men at the Ivy League.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:24.780
The gamers fought back.

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:27.200
This became known as Gamer-gate.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:32.220
The press has completely misunderstood it,
and the defamed the gamers.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:35.520
They were just defending a hobby that they
love.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:40.250
And I made a video about them in my Factual
Feminist series and defended the gamers and

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:42.970
then they started to call me Based Mom.

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.860
I am a mom.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:52.210
But being based means being authentic, genuine,
no-nonsense and cool, so that's good, and

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.200
so I'm Based Mom on Twitter.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.270
You, I've been talking about you and sending
out your things.

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.270
They call you Based Goddess.

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:03.520
Camille: Oh, for heaven's sakes, fantastic.

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:04.520
It's a wonderful compliment.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:06.170
Christina: So the gamers, they appreciate
what we're doing because they have defined

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:10.570
the battle all by themselves, and there are
subcultures out there.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:15.730
Groups of people, hobbyists who read comic
books, and now these gender critics are coming

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:16.730
in.

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:17.760
They're not just at the universities.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:21.980
They're in all of the subcultures, and turning
them into very unhappy places.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:25.980
But they made a mistake when they went after
the gamers because the gamers fought back,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:28.350
and the gamers know a thing or two about winning.

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:34.250
Camille: Well, the entire world of animation,
and video games, and cartooning, this is all

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:38.640
a pure imagination and it must be protected,
like art.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:45.790
Christina: It's the art, and the world of
gaming is diverse, and if groups of women...there

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:47.500
are a small group of very cool gamers.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:51.200
It's mostly guys and a small group of women,
but if women, I don't know.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:55.580
If they want video games about Downton Abbey
they can make them.

00:14:55.580 --> 00:15:02.160
Camille: You see when you're not exposed to
complex works of art, like Dostoevsky for

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:07.010
example then you end up with a simplistic
view of human life.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:08.240
You see this Utopian view.

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:12.630
Somehow everything's going to be like butterflies
and birds, and so on.

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:18.330
The dark side of human existence is completely
missing from these people's experience or

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:19.330
study.

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:23.710
Now our generation, I think was more realistic
because our parents had been part of the Depression,

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:26.640
World War II, the Holocaust was huge...

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:27.640
Christina: Absolutely.

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:28.640
Right.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:32.280
Camille: ...and so we had a sense of reality
about what is important and what is barbaric

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:34.610
in human history.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:41.920
This idea that you can micromanage reality
down to the pleasant is a Doris Day view of

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:43.050
existence.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:44.050
It's so reactionary.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:46.720
People don't realize it's sentimental.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.980
It's middlebrow and it does not belong on
the college campus.

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:50.990
Christina: They are sanctimonious.

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:55.760
The sanctimony alone is enough to drive you
mad.

00:15:55.760 --> 00:16:01.480
What the students are doing now and this is
at Oxford University...I think you know about

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:08.030
this...the young women are demanding trigger
warnings for anything that's violent or unpleasant

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:14.170
in a classroom, and so I'm thinking again,
trigger warnings in a history class because

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:15.170
you're going to study...?

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.170
Camille: Also law.

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:17.750
I think it also had to do something of law.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:18.750
Christina: Oh, yes.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:20.250
Camille: It had to do with rape cases.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:21.370
They had to have a trigger warning.

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:26.810
Christina: Well, there's a group of professors
at Harvard Law School who are opposed to it

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:31.480
because they have young women who are going
to be studying law for some of them to be

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:35.070
litigators, but they do not want to hear even
the word, violate.

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:40.880
They've been asked to excise upsetting terms.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:45.660
Well, what kind of world are we going to have
when these people graduate?

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:46.810
Camille: It is so regressive.

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:53.560
It's like the era of the Hollywood studio
production code when violence was also censored

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:54.800
on not just sex.

00:16:54.800 --> 00:17:01.330
And that's why Alfred Hitchcock's Psycho in
1960, it changed the modern film and opened

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:07.579
it to all kinds of representations of the
reality of human existence.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:14.679
I think that from what I can see, most feminists
seem to lack a sense of psychopathology.

00:17:14.679 --> 00:17:18.760
They don't understand the most extreme manifestations
of human psychology.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:21.620
So again, they think they can micromanage
everything.

00:17:21.620 --> 00:17:27.449
They think that they can somehow fine-tune
the entire world to be entirely safe.

00:17:27.449 --> 00:17:32.440
The world will never be entirely safe for
any human being, male or female.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:40.530
The idea that somehow a girl going out at
night alone, as long as everyone has graduated

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:44.430
from an elite school will be somehow protected
against an attack from a maniac...

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:46.080
Christina: From a maniac, from psychopaths...

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:47.080
Camille: Exactly.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:57.009
Christina: ...and that if you tell a young
woman, "You should not binge drink, and then

00:17:57.009 --> 00:18:00.090
go out and walk around.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.270
You're courting danger."

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:03.809
"Oh, well, tell men not to rape.

00:18:03.809 --> 00:18:04.850
Don't tell us what we have to wear."

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:05.850
Camille: It's called blaming the victim.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:10.639
If you try to give good advice to be street
smart about how you dress, and how you behave

00:18:10.639 --> 00:18:15.470
and to be aware of your environment, and know
how to handle yourself in a potentially dangerous

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:17.210
environment, that's called blaming the victim.

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:18.210
It's absolutely crazy.

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:22.120
Christina: That men aren't predators, but
there are predators among men.

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:25.830
Camille: There are truly insane people.

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:27.980
The human mind remains primitive.

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:34.190
The human mind remains half-animal, but of
course, if you completely remove biology from

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:35.280
your consideration...

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:36.280
Christina: Then you're going to end up...

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:38.840
Camille: What's the first thing they do with
this kind of Girl Scout code, they have a

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:40.429
Girl Scout view of how...

00:18:40.429 --> 00:18:43.870
Christina: It's all socially constructed,
so we can just re-engineer...

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:45.140
Camille: They're Utopians.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:46.350
They're in this fantasy world.

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:49.210
They think they can produce a sexual utopia.

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:54.690
But all of the excitement and interest of
sexual relations comes from potential danger.

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:55.690
Guess what?

00:18:55.690 --> 00:18:57.370
Gay men have known this for thousands of years.

00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:01.039
Gay men go out everywhere around the world,
and court danger and death every time they

00:19:01.039 --> 00:19:03.049
pick up a rough trade on the street.

00:19:03.049 --> 00:19:04.049
It happens all the time.

00:19:04.049 --> 00:19:06.370
Do you hear gay man complaining all the time?

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:11.440
Gay men trying to micromanage the entire world,
so they can pick up strangers in bus terminals

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:12.440
without danger?

00:19:12.440 --> 00:19:17.190
No, gay men find the danger a kick, now why
is it then that men have that kind of more

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:18.659
sophisticated sexuality?

00:19:18.659 --> 00:19:20.520
But no, girls are still children.

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:22.549
We must always protect them.

00:19:22.549 --> 00:19:23.840
They're tender flowers.

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.600
It is so regressive.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:26.940
This is not feminism.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:29.500
Christina: I know it.

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:32.289
Camille: Nor is it modernism, as you say Victorian.

00:19:32.289 --> 00:19:39.700
Christina: It's Victorian, and it's also feminism
is full of so many bad ideas that should have

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:46.850
been laughed out of...for example, this idea
of “the male gaze” and they use this,

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:49.440
"Woman is treated as object passive, he’s
the agent”…

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.799
Camille: It's Laura Mulvey's idea from the
1970's.

00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:52.799
Christina: Exactly.

00:19:52.799 --> 00:19:58.080
Camille: It's absolutely just a polluted film
criticism, and Laura Mulvey's a very nice

00:19:58.080 --> 00:19:59.080
person.

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:00.279
I've met her at the British Film Institute
in London.

00:20:00.279 --> 00:20:01.590
Christina: I think she changed a little bit.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:04.179
Camille: But she knows nothing about the visual
arts.

00:20:04.179 --> 00:20:08.990
You cannot just wade into film criticism and
start talking about the male gaze without

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:15.679
knowing something about the history of art,
and of perspective and of vision, and so on.

00:20:15.679 --> 00:20:18.600
The male gaze is a foolish idea, it should
long ago been discarded.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:24.139
Christina: And it's not as if in gay culture
we don't find the male gays.

00:20:24.139 --> 00:20:29.909
I mean gay males enjoy looking at pictures
of beautiful, idealized...

00:20:29.909 --> 00:20:33.100
Camille: And there you disproved a theory.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:38.549
Because gay men know when they look at a beautiful
young man that they are not creating some

00:20:38.549 --> 00:20:42.710
sort of powerful subordination on that man.

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:45.809
They know they're looking up in admiration
at that beauty.

00:20:45.809 --> 00:20:49.690
It's been part of Western art since the Greeks,
for heaven's sakes.

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:57.380
Christina: I mean men look at women, and yes,
they're enjoying it, but they're also often

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:58.380
intimidated.

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:03.600
Camille: Very intimidated, and biologically
it appears to be that the visual apparatus

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:07.320
is much more connected to male sexuality than
it is in female terms, and this is what women

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:08.930
don't understand.

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:13.320
They think they can put on every kind of sexy
costume and it's merely décor.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:15.700
That it does not carry its own message.

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:16.919
Christina: Right.

00:21:16.919 --> 00:21:20.460
Camille: Because they don't see things as
visually as men do.

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:24.549
I don't know whether I have the male gaze
or what, but I totally understand this.

00:21:24.549 --> 00:21:28.529
There's this charge in female sexuality.

00:21:28.529 --> 00:21:33.559
Young women today want to wield the power
of female sexuality without then accepting

00:21:33.559 --> 00:21:35.160
the consequences of it.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:41.139
Christina: That's right, and I think what
we're seeing now on the campus is we've had

00:21:41.139 --> 00:21:48.350
this hyper puritanism, this policing of sexuality
and then all of these functionaries, the apparatchiks,

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:55.279
the universities...I read the University of
North Carolina has 40 or 50 sexual harassment,

00:21:55.279 --> 00:21:56.279
sexual assault...

00:21:56.279 --> 00:21:57.279
Camille: Oh, my.

00:21:57.279 --> 00:21:58.279
What a waste of money.

00:21:58.279 --> 00:21:59.730
Christina: ...work shoppers and they're busy.

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:00.730
Camille: Parasites.

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:02.020
Christina: They go back and forth to conferences.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:03.600
They get new ideas.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:04.860
Camille: Horrible.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:11.320
Christina: It used to be that a lot of theories
they objected to, they were inspired by French

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:16.509
philosophy and there was a lot of obscure
nonsense.

00:22:16.509 --> 00:22:20.629
Now a lot of the scholarship is driven by
these apparatchiks, some with functionaries

00:22:20.629 --> 00:22:27.240
in university and their little findings, which
is all propaganda, advocacy research.

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:30.570
So it turns out that Swarthmore is a rape
culture.

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:32.000
Camille: Oh, for heaven's sakes.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:33.960
Christina: And where on earth are women more...

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:35.480
Camille: A Quaker school.

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:36.480
It's ridiculous.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:37.480
Christina: ...safer.

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:47.409
They now have, these sexual harassment, assault
counselors they have fact books and little

00:22:47.409 --> 00:22:52.950
videos, and they bring kids in for orientation,
and tell them they actually describe the campus,

00:22:52.950 --> 00:23:00.080
"It's as dangerous at Oberlin and at Wesleyan
as war-torn Congo."

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:02.350
Camille: It’s absurd.

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:08.950
Christina: So the young women are frightened,
so they've introduced so much paranoia.

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:10.880
Camille: Yeah, it's the bureaucrats.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:16.419
Christina: And the culture of blame and young
men can be destroyed simply by being accused

00:23:16.419 --> 00:23:17.720
in...

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:19.990
Camille: The kangaroo courts, absolutely.

00:23:19.990 --> 00:23:21.059
Christina: ...the kangaroo courts.

00:23:21.059 --> 00:23:23.129
Camille: The bureaucrats have taken over the
universities.

00:23:23.129 --> 00:23:29.620
The faculty also have very critically lost
power over the past 30 to 40 years.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:32.620
The faculty has been utterly passive and not
pushing back against all of this.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.299
Christina: Where have they been?

00:23:35.299 --> 00:23:36.830
When we were talking...

00:23:36.830 --> 00:23:37.830
Camille: Utter wimps.

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:41.030
Christina: ...20-21 years ago, I thought for
sure more scholars would come forward.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:50.519
Because I do believe that this panic, this
Salem witch trial atmosphere, it was created

00:23:50.519 --> 00:23:58.710
by scholars working with university functionaries,
but it's going to take scholars to stop it.

00:23:58.710 --> 00:23:59.710
Where are they?

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:00.820
Camille: Yes, that's right.

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:01.820
They're afraid.

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:02.820
There's a fear.

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:08.280
Now once you tangle with the bureaucrats,
they have all the power and now with federal

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:10.269
regulations behind it, it's intrusive.

00:24:10.269 --> 00:24:14.740
I'm hoping Congress is going to roll this
back, this over-interpretation of Title IX.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:20.139
Christina: Back in the 1990s, when we were
winning the argument, and we had the support

00:24:20.139 --> 00:24:29.450
of the media, there were all these great writers,
that took our side, pro free speech, and it

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:30.679
was taken for granted, and people would make
fun of the courses.

00:24:30.679 --> 00:24:35.259
They would look at the MLA and there would
be these silly courses on, I don't know...cross-dressing

00:24:35.259 --> 00:24:41.980
and medieval Valencia or autoeroticism in
the early Jane Austen.

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:47.090
It was fun, and then the media got a little
tired of it, and thought it had gone away.

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:48.520
It didn't go away.

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:49.520
It got much worse.

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:58.450
And if you look at the curriculum now and
the courses that kids take...on Twitter there's

00:24:58.450 --> 00:25:06.999
a Twitter called Peer Review and all he does
is report abstracts, show abstracts in journals.

00:25:06.999 --> 00:25:12.870
It's so shocking, and it never ends, and you'd
think they've reached the ultimate of madness.

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:18.690
Just to give an example and this isn't the
worst, Feminist Glaciology.

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:21.990
It's a feminist study of glaciers.

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:26.549
It draws on feminist post Colonialism, and
feminist political ecology.

00:25:26.549 --> 00:25:29.200
What are these fields?

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:31.820
There's a field, Feminist Political Ecology.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:33.039
What do they read?

00:25:33.039 --> 00:25:39.330
It must just be slogans, and mixed with a
little propaganda, a little paranoia thrown

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:40.909
in.

00:25:40.909 --> 00:25:44.619
One course after the other, so are they taking
History of Western Civilization?

00:25:44.619 --> 00:25:46.289
Are they learning about art?

00:25:46.289 --> 00:25:49.269
Do they know major historical figures?

00:25:49.269 --> 00:25:55.039
No, but they could talk about these arcane,
little propaganda, stick topics.

00:25:55.039 --> 00:26:00.799
Camille: Yes, the core curriculum was abandoned
as these new micro-fields came in.

00:26:00.799 --> 00:26:04.159
Women's studies, African-American studies
and so on, and identity politics, they changed

00:26:04.159 --> 00:26:10.369
the curriculum in the 1970s, and then all
of a sudden the whole history of Western art,

00:26:10.369 --> 00:26:13.720
well, that's classist, it's racist, so we
have to abandon that.

00:26:13.720 --> 00:26:19.470
And so what you have now is a whole generation,
in fact two generations probably of graduate

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:25.159
students who are incapable, actually of ever
teaching in a broad, narrative way, the old,

00:26:25.159 --> 00:26:26.179
wonderful survey courses.

00:26:26.179 --> 00:26:27.179
Christina: Right.

00:26:27.179 --> 00:26:32.490
Camille: Now what I'm calling for is an attack
on upon the curriculum, a reduction by 60%

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:39.009
of these elected, these silly electives to
be thrown out, and a compulsory return to

00:26:39.009 --> 00:26:46.049
teaching within these core ideas that are
oriented toward ancient to modern.

00:26:46.049 --> 00:26:50.320
Because another thing that, in addition to
what you just mentioned, the fragmentation,

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:56.379
is that too many courses now are dated in
the present, the present from after the Enlightenment

00:26:56.379 --> 00:26:58.270
or even after the 19th century.

00:26:58.270 --> 00:27:03.660
So you have an imbalance in the curriculum,
so that young people have no sense of the

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:08.760
ancient civilizations, and the way they rose
and fell over time.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.270
They have no idea of the world.

00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:15.019
They think that all the evils of the world
are isolated in Western culture.

00:27:15.019 --> 00:27:21.529
They have no idea about the atrocities, and
the oppressions of empires all over the world.

00:27:21.529 --> 00:27:28.480
So what we need to do I discovered in my students
coming from a broad range of preparations,

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:31.619
now and then there'll be private schools,
but mostly they come from good suburban schools

00:27:31.619 --> 00:27:37.690
or from bad inner city schools and they know
absolutely nothing about the chronology of

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:39.080
human life.

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:43.730
Hence, they look around them, and they see
things that are flaws in our current system,

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:47.470
and they believe that these flaws can be fixed,
and that all other cultures in the history

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:52.940
of the world were somehow a paradise on earth,
and we're enjoying the most liberties, ever

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:53.940
as women.

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:57.399
Christina: These cultures and all these students
who are so hostile to the United States because

00:27:57.399 --> 00:28:05.100
it's a capitalist, hetero, patriarchal, oppressive,
imperialist system, these sorts of terms are

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:06.499
taken seriously.

00:28:06.499 --> 00:28:08.549
Well, compared to what?

00:28:08.549 --> 00:28:09.549
What other country?

00:28:09.549 --> 00:28:17.110
We're far from perfect, but we're a struggling
democracy that's done more to address racism,

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:22.620
and classism, and sexism than any country
I'm aware of.

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:24.140
But it's almost as if they don't know that.

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:25.140
Camille: I know it.

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:32.100
Christina: They don't know that we have experimented
with systems that don't have free markets

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:34.059
and they didn't work out very well.

00:28:34.059 --> 00:28:36.080
These students don't seem to know that.

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:37.259
They don't know the history of Communism.

00:28:37.259 --> 00:28:41.000
Camille: Oh, they have no sense of economics
or much less, political theory.

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:43.000
Christina: Economic illiteracy.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:49.029
And so they're very confidently and they've
been taught in their classes, and what astonishes

00:28:49.029 --> 00:28:53.809
me is someone who as a professor of many years,
I always thought it was a sacred commandment,

00:28:53.809 --> 00:28:56.619
"Thou shalt teach both sides of the argument."

00:28:56.619 --> 00:29:01.759
So in any philosophy class, I would make sure
whatever we were studying, I'd try to bring

00:29:01.759 --> 00:29:04.320
the best that was thought and said on both
sides.

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:12.010
Well, in these classrooms, in many classrooms
and especially anthropology, sociology, not

00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:14.970
philosophy...philosophy is so far immune to
this, although it has other problems...

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:16.200
Camille: Literature, throughout the humanities.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:21.399
Christina: ...literature, and then of course,
gender studies, all the ethnic studies classes

00:29:21.399 --> 00:29:24.070
it's largely one point of view.

00:29:24.070 --> 00:29:28.230
They have their doctrinal feuds, but it's
the gamut from A to B, they leave out all

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:29.690
other perspectives.

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:33.690
Conservatives aren't there, heaven forbid,
libertarian perspectives, not there.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:40.669
Commonsense has no place, and so the students
have this sense that they've been exposed

00:29:40.669 --> 00:29:47.210
to what they take to be settled knowledge.

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:48.210
It's not settled.

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:52.470
Camille: Well, this is another thing I'm calling
for is that the profession has not paid sufficient

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:55.530
attention to this question of advocacy in
the classroom.

00:29:55.530 --> 00:30:00.690
I think that it's important that teacher's
have opinions inside the classroom.

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:05.399
However they have an obligation, an ethical
and professional obligation, to identify what

00:30:05.399 --> 00:30:11.850
they're saying as opinion and to state openly
to the class that every person in the class,

00:30:11.850 --> 00:30:16.799
has the right to express a contrary opinion
and to hold it without the rest of the class

00:30:16.799 --> 00:30:21.950
scorning it or creating a hostile environment,
etc., but that's totally gone from the profession.

00:30:21.950 --> 00:30:27.309
The idea, the ethics of teaching, the ethics
of teaching, as well as the ethics of research

00:30:27.309 --> 00:30:30.789
are no longer a subject of study by the profession
itself.

00:30:30.789 --> 00:30:36.559
Christina: Oh, this is very important to bring
back, and just in terms of effective teaching

00:30:36.559 --> 00:30:40.460
what I'd love to do because I loved it as
a student...you'll remember this.

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:43.470
This was especially true in philosophy.

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:48.749
You would read a set of philosophers, let's
suppose philosophers defending free will in

00:30:48.749 --> 00:30:53.230
a metaphysics course, and either you'd tend
to agree with them as you read them, then

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:57.419
the teacher would switch and you'd read the
determinist, and the critiques, and then you

00:30:57.419 --> 00:30:59.429
would say, "How did I ever believe that?"

00:30:59.429 --> 00:31:05.539
And you'd have that wonderful, intellectual
experience of changing your mind or realizing

00:31:05.539 --> 00:31:06.559
that, "I'd been wrong.

00:31:06.559 --> 00:31:07.559
Now I'm right.

00:31:07.559 --> 00:31:08.559
Maybe I'm wrong."

00:31:08.559 --> 00:31:16.210
Then you develop this sense of some humility
that you have to take into account a full

00:31:16.210 --> 00:31:17.240
range of opinion.

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:21.340
This is what I did in my philosophy class
and this is what every textbook I ever saw

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:23.660
in philosophy does.

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:29.110
But on one occasion, this is probably what
prompted me to start criticizing feminism,

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:34.080
the chair of my department asked me to teach
a Women's Studies course, Feminist Theory

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:35.320
and I sent away for textbooks.

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:36.320
I thought, "Okay.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:37.320
I'll teach it.

00:31:37.320 --> 00:31:38.320
I'm a feminist.

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:39.320
I'm a philosopher.

00:31:39.320 --> 00:31:41.639
I'll teach feminist philosophy."

00:31:41.639 --> 00:31:47.609
I assumed, I don't know what I thought, I
thought it would be historical figures saying

00:31:47.609 --> 00:31:53.100
some terrible things and some good things,
and then I thought maybe it was people pro

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:57.190
and con abortion or surrogate motherhood,
affirmative action.

00:31:57.190 --> 00:32:02.179
No, what is was, was a series of rather paranoid
texts.

00:32:02.179 --> 00:32:07.799
These were feminist philosophers writing these
really...but they were ideologues.

00:32:07.799 --> 00:32:15.419
The readings were mutually reinforcing and
there was a lot of false data.

00:32:15.419 --> 00:32:19.710
That's where I became...I alerted to the role
of propaganda.

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:24.049
You take these sensitive, mostly young women
in these Women's Studies classes they hear

00:32:24.049 --> 00:32:29.940
that one in four of them will be raped, that
they're going to cheated out of 25% of their

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:34.609
salary and they have a massive loss of self-esteem,
and that's only if they're not already dead

00:32:34.609 --> 00:32:40.270
from an eating disorder caused by patriarchal
standards of beauty.

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:41.629
They learn this.

00:32:41.629 --> 00:32:48.109
These sorts of things were in the textbooks,
and I was just appalled at the lack of balance,

00:32:48.109 --> 00:32:51.460
and as I said, the propaganda and the ideology.

00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:56.809
So that's when I went to the American Philosophical
Association criticizing it, and I thought

00:32:56.809 --> 00:33:01.720
like at other times I'd been there, you argue.

00:33:01.720 --> 00:33:06.200
It's combat, and I was prepared for that and
then you go out for drinks.

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:07.340
That's typically what happens.

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:08.970
Well, we did not go out for drinks.

00:33:08.970 --> 00:33:12.980
The room was full of feminist philosophers.

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:19.629
Some were hissing, stamping their feet, and
I was excommunicated from a religion, I didn't

00:33:19.629 --> 00:33:20.840
know existed.

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:24.440
And then it was the year or two after that,
I think I was reading The New York Times,

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:33.309
it was about this woman, Camille Paglia, I
wasn't sure how to pronounce it, and I was

00:33:33.309 --> 00:33:37.909
riveted and soon after you spoke at MIT.

00:33:37.909 --> 00:33:40.289
It was a rainy night.

00:33:40.289 --> 00:33:45.359
I went out to Brookline, and I had a little
child at home, and there was nothing to keep

00:33:45.359 --> 00:33:49.529
me away and the room was filled, all of them
MIT.

00:33:49.529 --> 00:33:50.730
Camille: Thousands of people came down.

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:55.980
Christina: They had video screens in the hall,
so students could see.

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:57.309
Camille: People came from all over in the
Boston area.

00:33:57.309 --> 00:33:58.309
Christina: We were there.

00:33:58.309 --> 00:33:59.769
Camille: They had the police there to handle
the crowd.

00:33:59.769 --> 00:34:06.889
Christina: Yeah, they had the police there,
and I think that evening, you may have deprogrammed

00:34:06.889 --> 00:34:07.889
a thousand students.

00:34:07.889 --> 00:34:10.110
You were there, and you were just telling
the truth.

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:13.240
Camille: Well, half of the crowd was against,
half was for.

00:34:13.240 --> 00:34:16.370
I felt like I was steering a ship on a stormy
sea.

00:34:16.370 --> 00:34:18.870
I did some of my best improv comedy ever that
night.

00:34:18.870 --> 00:34:19.870
Christina: Oh, you were...

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:20.870
Camille: I was putting on all...

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:22.480
Christina: If only we had that on tape.

00:34:22.480 --> 00:34:24.920
These days we'd have you on YouTube, get millions
of views.

00:34:24.920 --> 00:34:27.180
Camille: There's a transcript of it in my
Sex Art book.

00:34:27.180 --> 00:34:31.191
Christina: It was one of the most thrilling,
intellectual experiences in my life, and for

00:34:31.191 --> 00:34:37.370
all those students, and I remember there were
a group of people, I forget, they had a name

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:38.370
for themselves.

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:44.700
They were all dressed in black, and they came
to protest your views, I guess, on date rape

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:48.400
or something and they came, and they were
going to make trouble.

00:34:48.400 --> 00:34:53.170
But then they were so outnumbered, and they
were mesmerized by you, and I watched as they

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:55.100
quietly melted away.

00:34:55.100 --> 00:35:01.540
Camille: You see because they were very used
to disrupting speakers who read prepared texts,

00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:06.860
and I do improv in controversial situations,
and it's very difficult to deal with because

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:09.950
everything negative that came from the floor,
I would just do something with it and throw

00:35:09.950 --> 00:35:10.950
it right back.

00:35:10.950 --> 00:35:12.280
Christina: Well, that's the way to do it.

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:15.510
Camille: I was trained in the school of Joan
Rivers at that point.

00:35:15.510 --> 00:35:17.360
Christina: Oh, that's the way to do it.

00:35:17.360 --> 00:35:19.900
As God is my witness, I will not read a speech
again.

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:22.340
It's actually not a good idea.

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:24.040
It's better just to talk to the audience.

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:25.390
So this is what happened.

00:35:25.390 --> 00:35:33.210
A couple weeks ago, I went to UMass, Amherst
and I was there with Milo Yiannopoulos, who

00:35:33.210 --> 00:35:42.720
is this provocateur, satirist, self-described
shit poster and just funny and he says provocative

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:48.060
things to me, not to everyone and I have to
correct him.

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:49.060
He does go too far.

00:35:49.060 --> 00:35:51.940
So he's sort of like it's not really good
cop, bad cop.

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:58.230
It's more like concerned mother and wayward
son, and we've gone to a few campuses together

00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:01.220
and then we had a comedian, this wonderful
comedian, Steve Crowder.

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:02.690
So there were three of us.

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:11.030
As soon as we come on, people in the audience
just burst out, they start screeching, and

00:36:11.030 --> 00:36:15.840
yelling, and then Milo says something and
he's doing what you say.

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:22.930
He makes some...I won't repeat what Milo said,
but that provoked him, and then I went up

00:36:22.930 --> 00:36:27.370
as Based Mom to be reasonable, I thought.

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.250
Well, I upset them.

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:30.870
They were screaming even more.

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:32.790
They were using foul language.

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:36.180
It's all on tape, so it's gone viral.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:41.780
But there was not a moment that they weren't
screaming, and I don't know where the campus

00:36:41.780 --> 00:36:42.780
police were.

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:48.320
Apparently this is permitted, but at one point
Milo said, "We're not going away.

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:49.320
We're here.

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:52.110
We're going to give our talk," and then the
comedian did this little riff.

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:56.680
He was angry at them and that's also gone
viral on the internet.

00:36:56.680 --> 00:37:05.110
But my hope is that...so this has all been
covered by the conservative press, not a word

00:37:05.110 --> 00:37:11.070
in The New York Times, not a word in The New
Republic or on Slate.

00:37:11.070 --> 00:37:18.880
The liberal media is not paying attention,
so I'm very concerned.

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:24.320
I mean the last time we had the help of the
liberal media that didn't work, so maybe not

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:26.680
having them, maybe it doesn't matter.

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:33.140
But I'm just wondering who's going to come
forward and defend just free speech.

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:35.280
Where is that right now?

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:36.280
Where is the movement?

00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:37.280
Where are the Nat Hentoff's?

00:37:37.280 --> 00:37:41.990
Well, Nat Hentoff is still there, but in those
days we had just so many people coming forward

00:37:41.990 --> 00:37:43.360
and defending free speech.

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:44.590
Then you had people...

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:49.720
Camille: I think there has to be pressure
on the colleges and universities themselves,

00:37:49.720 --> 00:37:57.050
to maintain an atmosphere for civil discourse
and for hospitality to guests of any political

00:37:57.050 --> 00:38:02.540
persuasion, and without that kind of backbone
coming from the college administrators themselves,

00:38:02.540 --> 00:38:04.010
I think this is going on.

00:38:04.010 --> 00:38:08.780
But I feel that there's slowly a sense of
things turning.

00:38:08.780 --> 00:38:10.310
The seriousness of it is starting.

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:11.310
To become aware...

00:38:11.310 --> 00:38:12.310
Christina: I think so, too.

00:38:12.310 --> 00:38:14.600
Camille: ...that this is really getting absurd
now.

00:38:14.600 --> 00:38:21.020
Christina: And I also think some of the...not
some...I think a lot of the millennials, because

00:38:21.020 --> 00:38:25.741
it's a scrappy generation, and it's also they're
all connected on the internet and so they

00:38:25.741 --> 00:38:28.780
talk to each other we haven't heard from them
yet.

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:33.110
I think every generation faces a threat to
freedom.

00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:34.730
You always have to fight for it.

00:38:34.730 --> 00:38:41.320
The '50s, you had the McCarthy era and earlier
generations, there's always movements that

00:38:41.320 --> 00:38:46.870
come along, and have good reasons why someone
should have to shut up and keep quiet, so

00:38:46.870 --> 00:38:49.510
these censorship campaigns.

00:38:49.510 --> 00:38:57.720
So this generation is being challenged, and
I think I'm seeing signs of rebellion.

00:38:57.720 --> 00:39:03.330
Camille: I don't think students realize the
kind of power that they have for pushback

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:05.380
against the college administrations.

00:39:05.380 --> 00:39:09.690
They keep on accepting all of this paternalism,
this suffocating, all enveloping paternalism.

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:15.870
Christina: Right, I think the activists, the
social justice warriors on campus they know

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:17.910
the power they have over administrators.

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:18.910
Camille: Yes, that's right.

00:39:18.910 --> 00:39:25.420
Christina: Someone taped a young woman going
into an office at Vassar College demanding

00:39:25.420 --> 00:39:27.390
a copy of the...it was a setup.

00:39:27.390 --> 00:39:31.430
There was someone filming this poor administrator,
really.

00:39:31.430 --> 00:39:36.600
So this pretend student went in with a copy
of the U.S. Constitution, I think it was Vassar

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:41.400
and she wanted it shredded because it was
triggering.

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:46.080
And the conservative media had a good time
with it because it was showing that here's

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:49.430
an administrator that would shred the Constitution.

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:50.770
That's not what I saw in the tape.

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:54.130
I saw a frightened administrator.

00:39:54.130 --> 00:39:59.250
Terrified of this young woman, probably thinking,
"Am I going to lose my job if this doesn't...?"

00:39:59.250 --> 00:40:01.740
and she was a hostage.

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:09.150
This administrator was like a frightened hostage,
and this is what I think, so what I want the

00:40:09.150 --> 00:40:17.170
millennials to understand, the social justice
activists and the Safe Space people on campus,

00:40:17.170 --> 00:40:23.390
they are pressuring and frightening the administrators
who are giving into them which is nothing

00:40:23.390 --> 00:40:25.670
these administrators should be proud of.

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:33.440
But it's time for a pushback from the students
who value free speech, who value rational

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:40.350
scholarship, who would like to be free individuals,
and to stop the policing of sexuality.

00:40:40.350 --> 00:40:42.440
All of these things have to be fought for
again.

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:44.160
Camille: That's right, absolutely.

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:48.820
Yeah, so there has to be a new movement, a
new Free Speech Movement in my view.

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:49.980
There has to be.

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:52.910
It has to come from the students, as it did
the first time with Mario Savio at Berkeley.

00:40:52.910 --> 00:40:57.680
Christina: I was just going say we need the
Mario Savio's, and at that time they had a

00:40:57.680 --> 00:41:01.830
lot to lose too, because that's what the students
will tell you.

00:41:01.830 --> 00:41:08.260
They'll tell you, "Your college is so expensive,
and if I get in trouble..." and now you can

00:41:08.260 --> 00:41:16.430
be marked, as a rape cultural apologist or
a racist simply by challenging, simply by

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:20.990
asking for open debate, you can be...there's
a lot of stigma.

00:41:20.990 --> 00:41:25.880
Camille: Well, the administrators, they are
now running essentially a business.

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:28.610
The colleges are all competitive with each
other.

00:41:28.610 --> 00:41:32.660
They're desperate for tuition dollars, so
the bureaucrats are all about protecting the

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:35.460
PR image of the university.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:40.200
So they want to be on the politically correct
side of protecting those who have suffered

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:43.460
from racial or gender injustice in the past,
etc.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:48.680
There really is this...speak of sanctimonious,
there's this puffed up view of themselves.

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:54.610
They're somehow part of the progressive movement
of social justice in the universe, by suppressing

00:41:54.610 --> 00:41:56.850
people who have dissident views on campus.

00:41:56.850 --> 00:42:02.910
Christina: You can't even get...and I've been
writing about this for years, as you know.

00:42:02.910 --> 00:42:10.520
There's now this set of...it's just a body
of egregiously false information at the heart

00:42:10.520 --> 00:42:15.481
of campus radicalism, particularly campus
feminism, and 21 years ago we were talking

00:42:15.481 --> 00:42:18.641
about false statistics on rape, false statistics
on the wage gap.

00:42:18.641 --> 00:42:20.990
This wage gap is still going on.

00:42:20.990 --> 00:42:22.490
Camille: It will never go away.

00:42:22.490 --> 00:42:23.490
It's so ridiculous.

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:27.600
Christina: This one statistician said a phony
statistic is harder to kill than a vampire

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:28.600
once it's out.

00:42:28.600 --> 00:42:31.970
A false feminist statistic is the hardest
to kill of all.

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:32.970
Camille: It's unbelievable.

00:42:32.970 --> 00:42:35.660
Christina: It is beyond the reach of rational
analysis.

00:42:35.660 --> 00:42:40.460
Camille: No one understands that, for heaven's
sakes, John Kennedy signed the Equal Pay bill,

00:42:40.460 --> 00:42:42.550
it was like decades ago.

00:42:42.550 --> 00:42:48.440
It was a half century ago and there are very
rare cases of a woman being paid less than

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:50.120
a man for doing the same job.

00:42:50.120 --> 00:42:51.220
Christina: The same job.

00:42:51.220 --> 00:42:52.220
Camille: Okay.

00:42:52.220 --> 00:42:53.220
Right, the same job.

00:42:53.220 --> 00:42:54.220
They don't understand this.

00:42:54.220 --> 00:42:55.350
It's so simple, a simple point.

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:56.350
Christina: No responsible economist would
tell you otherwise.

00:42:56.350 --> 00:42:57.570
Camille: Oh, my Lord.

00:42:57.570 --> 00:43:03.210
Christina: Yet, when I say this on campus,
shrieks.

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:09.730
And some young woman at Oberlin I remember
as saying, "If you want to make as much as

00:43:09.730 --> 00:43:16.260
a man, you should change your major because
on average men are more likely to study these

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:22.260
highly remunerative fields, petroleum engineering
or naval architecture."

00:43:22.260 --> 00:43:26.520
And I just said to the Oberlin student, "You
know if you study feminist dance therapy you

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:27.970
might not earn as much."

00:43:27.970 --> 00:43:33.470
Oh, my goodness, they were outraged at the
idea that they couldn't study what they wanted,

00:43:33.470 --> 00:43:36.200
and then earn as much and so forth.

00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:40.200
They carry on, but it's we've got this propaganda.

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:44.980
There's no way to correct it and then you
get shouted down on campus, if you even suggest...

00:43:44.980 --> 00:43:50.430
Camille: Well, the biggest gap in Women's
Studies is the failure to have a requirement

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:51.430
about biology.

00:43:51.430 --> 00:43:52.430
Christina: Oh, well that.

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:53.430
Camille: Because there's no reference to biology.

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:59.280
You've had 40 years of Women's Studies with
a social constructionist view of gender without

00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:02.590
the slightest reference to hormones or endocrinology
or anything else.

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:03.590
Christina: Exactly.

00:44:03.590 --> 00:44:05.971
Forty years of Women's Studies, and I think
we know less about gender than we did at the

00:44:05.971 --> 00:44:09.210
start of it, for this very reason.

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:13.690
This dogma, oh, that men and women are the
same, that we're cognitively interchangeable.

00:44:13.690 --> 00:44:17.890
Camille: We're blank slates at birth, and
society inscribes gender on us.

00:44:17.890 --> 00:44:18.890
It's absurd.

00:44:18.890 --> 00:44:23.840
Christina: As one feminist philosopher said
many years ago, "We're all born bisexual,

00:44:23.840 --> 00:44:29.440
and then through socialization, we're transformed
into gendered human beings, one destined to

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:32.370
command, the other to obey."

00:44:32.370 --> 00:44:36.410
I once read that to my husband who said, "Which
one commands and which obeys?"

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:37.600
Camille: There you go.

00:44:37.600 --> 00:44:41.470
Christina: For heaven's sakes, I'm someone
who was writing a dissertation on androgyny

00:44:41.470 --> 00:44:46.390
and I never for one moment in my entire life
have questioned that the sexes are actually

00:44:46.390 --> 00:44:52.000
different, and that there's a very powerful,
hormonal compulsion that drives the sexes

00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:54.110
together for our procreation, hello, okay.

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:57.140
Camille: And it's so obvious and people say,
"Well, what's the evidence?"

00:44:57.140 --> 00:45:04.720
Well, let's start with the entire anthropological
record or just look at a magazine rack and

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:08.740
it's clear that men are looking, on average,
I'm not talking about all men and all women,

00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:11.200
but on average, there are different propensities.

00:45:11.200 --> 00:45:16.420
Christina: Men have 8 to 10 times the amount
of testosterone circulating in their bodies

00:45:16.420 --> 00:45:17.550
than women do.

00:45:17.550 --> 00:45:22.720
As if there aren't consequences, but of course,
this subject is something utterly untouched

00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:23.790
in gender studies.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:27.700
You're going to graduate with a degree in
Women's Studies and know nothing about it.

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:28.700
Camille: Nothing about it.

00:45:28.700 --> 00:45:29.700
Christina: Nothing.

00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:33.950
Camille: Nothing to know and so they are,
at any time they find statistical disparity

00:45:33.950 --> 00:45:39.330
between men and women, any field, if there
are more male petroleum engineers, it has

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:40.330
to be discrimination.

00:45:40.330 --> 00:45:42.870
Camille: Right, it can't be women's free choice
for any reason.

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:46.340
Christina: Free choice and that on average
women are interested in other things.

00:45:46.340 --> 00:45:50.650
Camille: Absolutely and also women want more
flexibility in their lives to allow for children...

00:45:50.650 --> 00:45:51.650
Christina: Children.

00:45:51.650 --> 00:45:52.650
Camille: ...but that's not also part of the
feminist equation.

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:58.220
Christina: Oh, and if we don't have a special
bond with children...It's so absurd the denial

00:45:58.220 --> 00:46:01.390
of nature, the denial of femininity and masculinity.

00:46:01.390 --> 00:46:02.390
Camille: Absolutely.

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:11.510
Christina: Which for most people, it's a source
of enjoyment and if you are conventionally

00:46:11.510 --> 00:46:17.530
feminine, you enjoy that typically and the
same with male, and you enjoy a masculine

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:18.530
man.

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:25.790
And all of this is now either denied or there's
this aura of disapproval around conventional

00:46:25.790 --> 00:46:32.160
sexuality and there's so little pushback in
the Academy.

00:46:32.160 --> 00:46:39.430
Camille: Well, it's because there's no knowledge,
and people are just settled and sluggish within

00:46:39.430 --> 00:46:45.310
their little selves, their ideological selves
and there really are very few truly inquiring

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:49.911
minds left, no true intellectuals, really
in the Academy.

00:46:49.911 --> 00:46:54.170
The Academy and, of course the colleges and
universities, have never been known for producing

00:46:54.170 --> 00:46:56.080
free thinkers.

00:46:56.080 --> 00:46:59.770
It is after all a bureaucratic entity the
Academy.

00:46:59.770 --> 00:47:05.510
If we want intellectuals, the present system
is certainly not the way to produce them.

00:47:05.510 --> 00:47:09.190
Christina: I'm just wondering, "What is this
generation?

00:47:09.190 --> 00:47:13.550
Are we going to have some great public intellectuals
coming forth, who aren't just spouting dogma?"

00:47:13.550 --> 00:47:18.990
Camille: You'd think that the web would allow
people to have free and independent though,

00:47:18.990 --> 00:47:19.990
but no.

00:47:19.990 --> 00:47:21.630
Christina: No, you have all these people...

00:47:21.630 --> 00:47:23.234
Camille: Yeah, everyone is so grouped and
clustered.

00:47:23.234 --> 00:47:25.820
Christina: ...and these little Twitter moms
that are policing everyone.

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:26.820
Camille: Oh, absolutely.

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:29.450
Christina: You'll have these incidents.

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:34.450
This poor comet scientist who wore the wrong
shirt to a press conference, and then they

00:47:34.450 --> 00:47:40.920
were hysterical on the internet, to punish
him and censor him, and these kinds of happen.

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:42.030
Camille: Parochialism of their minds.

00:47:42.030 --> 00:47:43.830
The banality of their minds.

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:45.410
Christina: I know exactly what you're saying.

00:47:45.410 --> 00:47:47.010
Why aren't there more people like us?

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:48.270
Why don't more people see it?

00:47:48.270 --> 00:47:50.980
Camille: Well, we're the product of a certain
time.

00:47:50.980 --> 00:47:55.490
I realize that now and I'm trying actually
not to be too hard on the young people today

00:47:55.490 --> 00:47:57.960
because they didn't have our kind of background.

00:47:57.960 --> 00:47:59.320
Our parents...

00:47:59.320 --> 00:48:03.180
Christina: The comedians, Mort Saul and Lenny
Bruce, and then George Gavin.

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:04.880
Camille: Carlin, comedians were tremendous.

00:48:04.880 --> 00:48:09.090
Christina: Richard Pryor and so today the
comedians aren't allowed on campus.

00:48:09.090 --> 00:48:10.290
Camille: Yes, that's right.

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:11.290
Christina: They're triggering.

00:48:11.290 --> 00:48:12.290
They don't want to go.

00:48:12.290 --> 00:48:13.940
Camille: Humor comes from violating taboos.

00:48:13.940 --> 00:48:19.510
All right, we could have a taboo about women
or race or anything else, then you you're

00:48:19.510 --> 00:48:21.900
attracting humor toward it.

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:27.250
Any rigidity attracts humor because satire
breaks up the rigidity.

00:48:27.250 --> 00:48:35.770
Christina: So anyway my question here is,
I think these propaganda and the denial that

00:48:35.770 --> 00:48:39.970
there are differences between the sexes they're
just built up.

00:48:39.970 --> 00:48:46.520
It's a big lie and it's almost to me, comparable...I
can't carry out this analogy too far.

00:48:46.520 --> 00:48:55.100
But the credit default swaps and there was
all of these bad mortgages distributed everywhere.

00:48:55.100 --> 00:48:57.900
No one knew where they were, but they were
everywhere in the economy and then it brought

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:01.900
it down, and I see this as feminist misinformation.

00:49:01.900 --> 00:49:08.150
We have feminist misinformation in every area,
disseminated by these credulous journalists

00:49:08.150 --> 00:49:09.480
who don't ask the right questions.

00:49:09.480 --> 00:49:13.690
So everybody believes sexes are exactly the
same and there's this wage gap.

00:49:13.690 --> 00:49:15.910
That there's rape culture on campus.

00:49:15.910 --> 00:49:20.290
That women are victims and men are...all of
this is out there.

00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:23.160
Large numbers of people believing something
that's just not true.

00:49:23.160 --> 00:49:26.610
Camille: Well, you have to realize that a
lot of people in the media now are products

00:49:26.610 --> 00:49:28.030
of those PC elite schools.

00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:29.470
I encounter them all the time.

00:49:29.470 --> 00:49:30.470
Christina: So do I.

00:49:30.470 --> 00:49:33.070
Camille: And trying to get their minds to
focus, they have an utterly...I'm talking

00:49:33.070 --> 00:49:34.980
about Yale degrees, Harvard degrees, Princeton
degrees.

00:49:34.980 --> 00:49:37.760
Christina: Oh, the more prestigious the degree,
the more likely it is.

00:49:37.760 --> 00:49:38.760
Camille: The worse, absolutely.

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:39.760
Their minds are like...

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:41.080
Christina: Whole generation of young women...

00:49:41.080 --> 00:49:42.080
Camille: ...they can't focus.

00:49:42.080 --> 00:49:44.300
They have no knowledge, and they're in media.

00:49:44.300 --> 00:49:47.690
They're writing The New York Times, they're
doing political analysis in The New York Times.

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:48.690
They know nothing.

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:51.120
Christina: They know nothing, but they have
a lot of confidence, very high self-esteem.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:52.170
Camille: Yes, because...

00:49:52.170 --> 00:49:53.170
Christina: No problem there.

00:49:53.170 --> 00:49:56.180
Camille: ...they have these bright and shiny
degrees, which are in fact, meaningless and

00:49:56.180 --> 00:49:57.180
valueless.

00:49:57.180 --> 00:49:58.180
Christina: Worthless.

00:49:58.180 --> 00:49:59.680
Camille: Worthless degrees, worthless Ivy
League degrees.

00:49:59.680 --> 00:50:00.960
Christina: They should go back.

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:02.130
They should go, I don't know.

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:06.500
Camille: Remember I used to say that Naomi
Wolf, her parents should have sued Yale for

00:50:06.500 --> 00:50:07.500
mal-education.

00:50:07.500 --> 00:50:08.883
Christina: Susan Faludi's parents should have
sued for...

00:50:08.883 --> 00:50:14.760
Camille: Susan Faludi and Naomi Wolf, these
are two very smart women, who were defrauded

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:16.590
by their elite school education.

00:50:16.590 --> 00:50:18.320
Look how their careers have tanked.

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:19.780
Why is that?

00:50:19.780 --> 00:50:22.900
They didn't have the solid, rigorous education
you and I did.

00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:26.470
I went to the State University of New York
at Binghamton.

00:50:26.470 --> 00:50:32.250
I had a superb education in the basics, and
that is fueling my productivity at my stage

00:50:32.250 --> 00:50:35.360
of life, but look at Susan Faludi and Naomi
Wolf.

00:50:35.360 --> 00:50:39.270
They started out so strong, and there's nothing
because they have nothing underneath it.

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:43.570
Christina: I identify with them in this way.

00:50:43.570 --> 00:50:50.191
I came from Southern California in the hippie
era, I was a flower child, so if I had come

00:50:50.191 --> 00:50:55.320
to college and they had courses in witchcraft
I was interested in that.

00:50:55.320 --> 00:50:59.660
My friend in high school was Mimi Simo [SP]
she became a witch.

00:50:59.660 --> 00:51:02.510
She was a brilliant girl, but she became Star
Hawk, and I don't know.

00:51:02.510 --> 00:51:06.200
We were friends, but then she became...maybe
we could still be friends.

00:51:06.200 --> 00:51:08.200
I wouldn't want her to put a hex on me.

00:51:08.200 --> 00:51:18.220
But I went to NYU, and if they'd had kooky
majors, I would have gone in for them.

00:51:18.220 --> 00:51:21.050
I would have been a witchcraft major maybe.

00:51:21.050 --> 00:51:25.990
But the most interesting thing I could find
was philosophy and my philosophy professors

00:51:25.990 --> 00:51:35.820
at NYU it was clear thinking and rigor and
precision rules, and coming as a flower...I

00:51:35.820 --> 00:51:39.320
thought it was far out.

00:51:39.320 --> 00:51:42.120
We read this book called Language, Truth,
and Logic by A.J.

00:51:42.120 --> 00:51:43.120
Ayer.

00:51:43.120 --> 00:51:50.670
It was a very extreme statement of the need
for a logical rigor in your thought, and so

00:51:50.670 --> 00:51:52.000
forth, and I loved it.

00:51:52.000 --> 00:51:59.050
I fell in love with it, and I slowly class
after class I had, reading the great philosophers,

00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:01.941
and I slowly became a disciplined thinker.

00:52:01.941 --> 00:52:09.980
I like to think that I did, but I always knew
how I once saw the world, and then the transformation.

00:52:09.980 --> 00:52:13.180
Of course, that's what I hoped to do in the
classroom.

00:52:13.180 --> 00:52:20.480
What I see now is that 17-year-old, 18-year-old
Christina coming from the California flower

00:52:20.480 --> 00:52:24.761
child culture, and a lot of kids are coming
from all sorts of backgrounds, but you come

00:52:24.761 --> 00:52:27.310
to school, it would be reinforced.

00:52:27.310 --> 00:52:34.410
It would be catered to, and I could have run
to the dean and said, "I'm not really getting

00:52:34.410 --> 00:52:36.450
the courses that I would like to take."

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:37.600
You can design your own major.

00:52:37.600 --> 00:52:39.240
Camille: That's right, it's cafeteria style
now.

00:52:39.240 --> 00:52:42.400
Christina: It's cafeteria style and it's even
in the museums now.

00:52:42.400 --> 00:52:44.020
Camille: Oh, absolutely.

00:52:44.020 --> 00:52:45.320
Ah, the museums, horrible.

00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:53.620
Christina: The museums, I was in Portland
and to the local museum, and there was a beautiful

00:52:53.620 --> 00:53:04.830
display of some Indian artifacts and some
exquisite photographs of Indians, Native Americans,

00:53:04.830 --> 00:53:07.230
and the works of art it was all beautiful.

00:53:07.230 --> 00:53:16.610
But in every room, there was some feminist
art historian had written a hectoring, little

00:53:16.610 --> 00:53:30.180
description censoring and talking about the
gaze of the tourist and all this kind of deconstruction,

00:53:30.180 --> 00:53:34.710
and so you couldn't learn about the art, and
you couldn't enjoy it.

00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:38.280
It had to be mediated through the mind of
this fanatical...

00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:39.340
Camille: Ideological perspective.

00:53:39.340 --> 00:53:44.800
It had to be filtered through, the judgementalism,
the moralism, the guilt tripping and so on.

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:46.360
Christina: It was one big guilt trip.

00:53:46.360 --> 00:53:50.710
Camille: Instead of the actual encounter with
an artifact from the past, and a sense of

00:53:50.710 --> 00:53:55.790
awe and reverence before that object, no we
have to have this interposed contemporary

00:53:55.790 --> 00:53:57.930
figure hectoring us.

00:53:57.930 --> 00:53:59.460
That's the sermon.

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:02.360
They're new preachers, the new scolds, perfect.

00:54:02.360 --> 00:54:03.720
Christina: They're scolds.

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:05.540
Camille: They're new scolds.

00:54:05.540 --> 00:54:06.900
Christina: They're scolds.

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:07.900
They're pests.

00:54:07.900 --> 00:54:15.330
So we see it going into the media, that's
a given, and now in the museums and then there's

00:54:15.330 --> 00:54:17.010
cultural appropriation.

00:54:17.010 --> 00:54:23.760
This is one of the new ideas that if you,
at some level a lot of this there's a kernel

00:54:23.760 --> 00:54:33.721
of wisdom, that you don't put on black face
and essentially demean another culture in

00:54:33.721 --> 00:54:34.721
that way.

00:54:34.721 --> 00:54:38.560
But now they're going after yoga classes and
that sort of thing, you're not supposed to

00:54:38.560 --> 00:54:39.560
say Namaste.

00:54:39.560 --> 00:54:41.630
That's going to put all of Portland out of
work.

00:54:41.630 --> 00:54:44.790
It's nothing but yoga studios, I don't know.

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:51.090
But now performances of the Mikado, the Gilbert
and Sullivan, people are saying, "Well, that

00:54:51.090 --> 00:54:52.480
was cultural appropriation.

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:53.480
It's demeaning."

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:59.470
You have the museum in Boston where they have
beautiful kimonos on display.

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:04.590
Well, now there were protesters and the protesters
didn't seem to be from Japan.

00:55:04.590 --> 00:55:07.520
It's almost always people that aren't in that
culture that are the most shrill.

00:55:07.520 --> 00:55:08.520
Camille: Oh, good Lord.

00:55:08.520 --> 00:55:11.840
Christina: And so the Boston Museum, the Museum
of Fine Arts had to apologize.

00:55:11.840 --> 00:55:13.240
Camille: Oh, I don't believe it.

00:55:13.240 --> 00:55:15.660
Christina: So this is going on.

00:55:15.660 --> 00:55:20.280
It's permeating the culture and I want it
to stop.

00:55:20.280 --> 00:55:21.800
Camille: Yes, that's right.

00:55:21.800 --> 00:55:24.770
It's so unsophisticated.

00:55:24.770 --> 00:55:25.770
This is not the way.

00:55:25.770 --> 00:55:26.770
Christina: Anti-intellectual.

00:55:26.770 --> 00:55:32.000
Camille: This is not the way you contemplate
history and art, through the contemporary

00:55:32.000 --> 00:55:33.610
political filter.

00:55:33.610 --> 00:55:37.120
This distorts the meaning of history and art.

00:55:37.120 --> 00:55:40.660
Christina: It distorts and trivializes, and
that's what I see with students.

00:55:40.660 --> 00:55:44.090
They're not having these encounters with great
works of art.

00:55:44.090 --> 00:55:45.090
Camille: Right.

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:46.090
Christina: That's what a teacher should do.

00:55:46.090 --> 00:55:48.100
Camille: We were produced by the great books
curriculum.

00:55:48.100 --> 00:55:52.260
Christina: We always had, no matter what even
if you had a bad teacher you always had a

00:55:52.260 --> 00:55:53.260
classic.

00:55:53.260 --> 00:55:59.100
Camille: And a sense of respect for the greatness
of the work, not this debunking, sneering

00:55:59.100 --> 00:56:00.100
deconstruction.

00:56:00.100 --> 00:56:01.191
Christina: Now they have a sense of superiority.

00:56:01.191 --> 00:56:02.191
Camille: Exactly.

00:56:02.191 --> 00:56:07.781
"Oh, this is like a product of all kinds of
outmoded or racial or gender paradigms," and

00:56:07.781 --> 00:56:08.781
so on.

00:56:08.781 --> 00:56:09.781
Give me a break.

00:56:09.781 --> 00:56:15.910
The whole history of the world is seen through
this contemporary lens, and again, it's absolutely...and

00:56:15.910 --> 00:56:20.910
that's we're not getting any important intellectuals
and young intellectuals, young writers, young

00:56:20.910 --> 00:56:22.330
analysts.

00:56:22.330 --> 00:56:26.330
If you're ever going to get any they have
got to start going back to the library and

00:56:26.330 --> 00:56:29.530
finding other independent thinkers because
they're certainly not being presented with

00:56:29.530 --> 00:56:30.530
them in the classroom.

00:56:30.530 --> 00:56:31.530
Christina: They're just not.

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:32.530
Camille: No.

00:56:32.530 --> 00:56:36.060
Christina: So that's the message to students
today.

00:56:36.060 --> 00:56:37.250
You cannot depend on your...

00:56:37.250 --> 00:56:39.020
Camille: Don't depend on the teachers.

00:56:39.020 --> 00:56:40.020
Christina: ...teachers.

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:41.990
Camille: No, distrust authority.

00:56:41.990 --> 00:56:47.210
Resist authority and go and find what you
need in the library and don't just sit there

00:56:47.210 --> 00:56:50.950
on rear end in front of the computer because
the computer self-selects for you.

00:56:50.950 --> 00:56:52.340
You're not going to find it.

00:56:52.340 --> 00:56:55.540
You have to go and physically engage with
the things on the shelves.

00:56:55.540 --> 00:56:56.540
It's what I did.

00:56:56.540 --> 00:57:00.100
I ransacked the Yale University Library.

00:57:00.100 --> 00:57:05.980
Christina: Someone called our universities,
"Islands of repression in a sea of freedom."

00:57:05.980 --> 00:57:08.020
Students have to liberate their islands.

00:57:08.020 --> 00:57:09.420
So we're here foment rebellion.

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:10.420
Camille: That's right.

00:57:10.420 --> 00:57:11.420
That's right.

00:57:11.420 --> 00:57:12.420
Christina: Well, thank you, Camille.

00:57:12.420 --> 00:57:13.900
Camille: Well, great, Christina, as usual.

00:57:13.900 --> 00:57:15.580
Christina: So I'm hoping we meet...

00:57:15.580 --> 00:57:16.700
Camille: We always agree.

00:57:16.700 --> 00:57:19.260
Christina: ...20 years from now and oh, goodness.

00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:20.330
Camille: That's right.

00:57:20.330 --> 00:57:21.330
Okay.

00:57:21.330 --> 00:57:24.060
Christina: It'll be free again and liberated.

00:57:24.060 --> 00:57:26.390
Camille: Let's hope free speech is restored.

00:57:26.390 --> 00:57:27.880
Christina: Yes, and sanity.

00:57:27.880 --> 00:57:28.440
Well, thank you

